# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
# ...but keep the shareable template
!.env.local.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts
client_secret*
WhatsApp*
split-screenshots/
pace-screenshots/
*.traineddata

# Raw plan exports. These carry the exporter's home coordinates in every
# weather snapshot, so they must never be committed. To refresh the bundled
# demo plan, run: node scripts/scrub-example-plan.mjs <export.json>
#
# Both names are listed: the app writes racepilot-plans-* now, and the old
# marathon-plans-* pattern stays so an export taken before the rename is still
# ignored. Never narrow this.
racepilot-plans-*.json
marathon-plans-*.json

# Strava (and other) account data exports. Same reason as above, only worse: an
# unpacked export is GPS traces that start at the athlete's front door, plus
# logins.csv, contacts.csv, followers.csv, profile.csv and messaging.json.
#
# Only activities.csv is ever read (see docs/activity-import.md) and it holds no
# coordinates at all, but the archive around it is not safe to have in a public
# repo. Keep exports outside the working tree where you can.
export-strava/
strava_export_*/
